a_answer:
For \(m\ge M_{0}\) split the sum as\n\[S_{m}=\sum_{n=1}^{M_{0}}|x_{n}|+\sum_{n=M_{0}+1}^{m}|x_{n}|.\]  
Using \(|x_{n+1}|\le \alpha|x_{n}|\) inductively gives \(|x_{n}|\le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\alpha^{\,n-M_{0}-1}\) for \(n\ge M_{0}+1\).  Therefore\n\[\sum_{n=M_{0}+1}^{m}|x_{n}|
   \le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\sum_{k=0}^{m-M_{0}-1}\alpha^{\,k}
   \le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\alpha^{\,k}
   =\frac{|x_{M_{0}+1}|}{1-\alpha}.\]  
Thus \(S_{m}\le C+\dfrac{|x_{M_{0}+1}|}{1-\alpha}\) for all \(m\ge M_{0}\), and the right‑hand side does not depend on \(m\).

a_question:
In the comparison test for series with non‑negative terms, suppose \((x_n)\) and \((y_n)\) satisfy \(y_n \ge x_n \ge 0\) for all \(n\). Explain why the two statements\n    \[
      \text{(i) If }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} y_n \text{ converges, then }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n \text{ converges,}
    \]\n    \[
      \text{(ii) If }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n \text{ diverges, then }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} y_n \text{ diverges,}
    \]\n    are logically equivalent. In your explanation, explicitly use the contrapositive and the fact that the terms are non‑negative.

a_answer:
For \(m\ge M_{0}\) split the sum as\n\[S_{m}=\sum_{n=1}^{M_{0}}|x_{n}|+\sum_{n=M_{0}+1}^{m}|x_{n}|.\]  
Using \(|x_{n+1}|\le \alpha|x_{n}|\) inductively gives \(|x_{n}|\le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\alpha^{\,n-M_{0}-1}\) for \(n\ge M_{0}+1\).  Therefore\n\[\sum_{n=M_{0}+1}^{m}|x_{n}|
   \le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\sum_{k=0}^{m-M_{0}-1}\alpha^{\,k}
   \le |x_{M_{0}+1}|\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\alpha^{\,k}
   =\frac{|x_{M_{0}+1}|}{1-\alpha}.\]  
Thus \(S_{m}\le C+\dfrac{|x_{M_{0}+1}|}{1-\alpha}\) for all \(m\ge M_{0}\), and the right‑hand side does not depend on \(m\).
